{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_20","type":"command_execution","command":"\"C:\\\\Program Files\\\\PowerShell\\\\7\\\\pwsh.exe\" -Command \"@'\nimport subprocess,tomllib\nc='06c615cbd3a75bde1826668e0b80f99d5306aabd'\ndef read(p): return subprocess.check_output(['git','show',c+':'+p]).decode('utf-8')\ns=read('docs/plans/2026-10-11-review-churn/README.md'); a=s.index('## 13.'); b=s.index('\\\\n## 14.',a); t=s[a:b]; print(t[-11500:])\nr=tomllib.loads(read('docs/requirements/interfaces.toml'))\nfor k,v in r.items():\n if isinstance(v,dict):\n  for name in ('IF-131','IF-263'):\n   if name in v: print(name,v[name])\n'@ | python -\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_20","type":"command_execution","command":"\"C:\\\\Program Files\\\\PowerShell\\\\7\\\\pwsh.exe\" -Command \"@'\nimport subprocess,tomllib\nc='06c615cbd3a75bde1826668e0b80f99d5306aabd'\ndef read(p): return subprocess.check_output(['git','show',c+':'+p]).decode('utf-8')\ns=read('docs/plans/2026-10-11-review-churn/README.md'); a=s.index('## 13.'); b=s.index('\\\\n## 14.',a); t=s[a:b]; print(t[-11500:])\nr=tomllib.loads(read('docs/requirements/interfaces.toml'))\nfor k,v in r.items():\n if isinstance(v,dict):\n  for name in ('IF-131','IF-263'):\n   if name in v: print(name,v[name])\n'@ | python -\"","aggregated_output":" mechanism, nothing new).\r\n    - **The lane never stalls.** A partial close is the only sanctioned stop, and a lane is never held (OI-70). The lane closes `partial` (`handback.py`): its work is committed with a keep/discard split, any code the bar refuses is quarantined as a bar-inert `.patch`, and an immutable report is written under `docs/handbacks/`. The branch merges and the run moves on to independent work.\r\n    - **An independent adjudicator judges the close, not the dependency** (`adjudicate-disposition.template.md`). It checks whether the claimed outcome is right and the keep/discard split honest, and it drafts the mandatory successor row.\r\n    - **The dependency question goes to the owner as an open item.** Where what is left is the owner's to rule, the successor carries an `[open_item]` card (one line, blast radius, options, recommendation). The mint makes it a pending OI and writes the OI into the successor's `needs`, so the successor sits queued but blocked until the owner rules.\r\n    - **The card cites the admission facts; it does not redo them.** The owner's scope rule keeps installability out of the adjudicator's judgement. So the handback report carries the builder's request, the gathered identity facts and, under `\"independent\"`, the admission session's reasons, and the card cites them instead of re-judging. The same OI gate serves a need found at planning under `\"human\"`: the queued WI's `needs` names the OI before any claim.\r\n  - **Revision 18: corrections from the revision 17 reviews, and decision 3's options.**\r\n    - **Decision 3, the owner's framing (confirmed in revision 19, below).** The owner expected one value, \"owner or delegate\": the session hands back, the need becomes an open item, and whatever runs the sequence rules it. That may be the owner, or another session the owner has blessed to take delegated decisions. Checked: a lane cannot tell whether it is attended. The only route from a lane to the owner is the partial close and its open item, on the owner's surface (`docs/open-items.html`, PROCESS.md �5). The delegated-decisions record is read afterwards, and the `Loop-Session` trailer marks loop-started commits but tells a lane nothing about who is attending. So \"delegate\" can only act at the ruling of the open item, never inside the lane, which makes the owner's single value the natural one. The options:\r\n      - **(A) Two values (recommended).** `dependency_admission = \"owner-or-delegate\"` is the shipped default: the lane hands back, and the open item is ruled by the owner or by a session acting on the owner's recorded delegation (PROCESS.md �4's shape for a held act: \"the delegation and its scope recorded\"). A delegate's ruling is the owner's act, recorded where the owner's ruling of an open item is recorded (corrected in revision 20; see the authority vocabulary below). `\"independent\"` lets a fresh admission session, never the requester, admit in the lane, attended or not. TOML spelling follows the file's lowercase, hyphenated values (`agent-iteration`, `escalate-first`).\r\n      - **(B) No key: derive it from `human_approval_through`,** as off-spine approvals already are (OI-30 D3), by mapping the ledger to `DevStg-Arch`. In this repo that makes admission independent by default, since that rung isn't held here. It ties supply-chain risk to spine maturity.\r\n      - **(C) A third value for an attended delegate inside the lane.** Dropped, because a lane can't tell, and nothing lets a lane reach a person but the open item.\r\n\r\n      Under every option, a `shipped` dependency is always the owner's. The naming of the shared vocabulary (\"owner\", as PROCESS.md �4 says, against `push`'s \"human\") is part of the confirmation.\r\n\r\n      **Confirmed by the owner (revision 19): option (A),** `dependency_admission = \"owner-or-delegate\" | \"independent\"`, with `\"owner-or-delegate\"` the shipped default, in the owner's own term.\r\n      - **What a delegate is** (owner, revisions 19�20). A delegate can do everything a human owner would do; the human has given an LLM agent explicit permission to act in their stead, and that permission grants whatever the human granted. The kit cannot enforce who is human, and no process can guarantee it: a session running outside the coordinator role and outside `agent-resume`, told it is delegated with full permission, cannot be told apart from the owner. So **owner and delegate are one role, \"owner-or-delegate\"**, and a delegate's act is the owner's act, recorded wherever the owner's would be (an open item's ruling, an approval's commit). It is not a delegated-decisions-record entry.\r\n      - **The distinction the kit can make, and keeps** (integrator, from the owner's answer; revision 20). What the kit *can* identify is an autonomous run: a session the kit's machinery starts, which is `agent-resume`'s loop, the dispatcher, and every worker they spawn. `kitlib/provenance.py` sets the `KIT_LOOP_SESSION` marker in that process (`mark_loop_process`), its children inherit it, and their commits must carry the `Loop-Session` trailer. **Checked: the coordinator role carries no marker**, attended or as a headless leg (`coordinator-tools/overnight_legs.py` sets none). The owner grouped the coordinator role with `agent-resume` as distinguishable; today the kit cannot tell a coordinator session from the owner, so the vocabulary entry below states which side it is on. That is what the existing enforcers already key on: `check.py`'s `held-status` step, the merge slot and the acceptance writer refuse such a session's move of a held status. So no enforcement code changes for delegation (this resolves Astra's revision 19 MAJOR on executable authority): the owner-or-delegate acts unmarked, as the owner does, and an autonomous run stays refused on held acts. The routing table in PROCESS_OPTIONS.md (\"a ruled open item � the exits � never decided under delegation\") is right for autonomous runs, and only its wording conflates them with delegation (Fable's revision 19 MAJOR).\r\n      - **The authority vocabulary, rewritten for consistency** (owner: \"some permissions / designations need to be rewritten � including some settings changing labelling\"; proposed, with item 10's TOML migration, as one RESYNC entry since adopters rename values):\r\n        - **PROCESS.md �4:** \"A held rung's approval is the owner's act, taken by the owner-or-delegate (anyone the owner has given that permission, human or agent; the kit cannot tell them apart). An autonomous run (a session the machinery starts, marked by its `Loop-Session` trailer) never approves on a held rung.\" The CLARITY re-attest case by an independent adjudicator stays as stated.\r\n        - **PROCESS_OPTIONS.md:** \"Delegated decisions record\" becomes the **autonomous decisions record**: the calls an autonomous run makes, recorded for the owner-or-delegate to confirm or overrule. Its routing table's \"never decided under delegation\" becomes \"never decided by an autonomous run\".\r\n        - **`process.toml` labels:** `push = \"human\"` becomes `\"owner-or-delegate\"`; the `decision_recording` comments' \"a DELEGATED run\" become \"an autonomous run\"; `human_approval_through`'s \"a human approves\" becomes \"the owner-or-delegate approves\", and whether the key itself is renamed (`owner_approval_through`) is part of the same entry. (Ruled in revision 35: the term is \"human-or-delegate\", and the key is renamed `human_or_delegate_approval_through`.)\r\n        - **Elsewhere:** status.md's `Needs <human>` bullets, the `Attest` and LLM-Attest wording, and the shipped prompts use the same two terms, owner-or-delegate and autonomous run.\r\n        - **The coordinator role** (this repo's `coordinator-cycle` skill, not kit machinery): either it is an owner-or-delegate by the owner's standing permission, which matches how it commits today, unmarked; or it is marked as an autonomous run when it runs headless, which would put its held acts behind the refusal. For the owner to choose. (Ruled in revision 35: an autonomous run when headless; built now, revision 36.)\r\n    - **The open-item card is assembled outside the adjudicator** (both reviewers). `adjudicate-disposition.template.md` makes the adjudicator author the card's options and recommendation, and intake refuses a card without a recommendation. Under the owner-or-delegate default no admission session ran, so the adjudicator would be judging installability, which the owner's scope rule forbids. So the disposition contract is amended: a dependency card is assembled from the builder's request and the gathered facts, the recommendation cell carrying the requester's case marked as the requester's (or, under `\"independent\"`, the admission session's reasons). The adjudicator carries it unchanged and judges only the close and its keep/discard split.\r\n    - **The decision binds the resolved lock, not one name** (Astra). Admitting a package before the lock is regenerated leaves every package the regeneration pulls in unjudged. So the candidate lock diff is resolved first, without installing; facts are gathered for every new or changed package; the decision names that exact set (names, versions, hashes); the install is checked against it; and a commit-versus-parent check couples any change to the dependency files with the act that admitted that set. **Stack-neutral** (revision 20, Fable): the act records the post-change content hash of each dependency file, and the shipped check compares file hashes; the package set stays in the act as text, since the kit can't parse every stack's lock. **Which act** (corrected in revision 23, Astra; revision 20 said \"a decision entry\" and \"the decision record is the one store\", which an owner-or-delegate ruling is not): under `\"owner-or-delegate\"`, the owner's ruling of the open item, recorded where the owner's rulings are; under `\"independent\"`, the admission session's entry in the autonomous decisions record. Each admission has exactly one such act, which holds the hashes once; an owner's ruling is never copied into the autonomous record.\r\n    - **The identity script is coordinator tooling** (Fable). `pip-audit` and `uv` are ledger rows at the `coordinator` tier, \"never forced on an adopter\". A shipped script calling them would move them to `shipped`, which needs an owner ruling. So the facts script lives with this repo's coordinator tooling; an adopter supplies its stack's equivalent; and with no facts (offline, or no tool), the answer is HAND-BACK.\r\n    - **The pre-review lock check is consistency, not freshness** (Astra). `lock-check.yml` regenerates the lock from the live index and fails if it moved, a scheduled heads-up by design. In a pre-review gate that would turn a valid pinned diff red on any upstream release. The gate checks that the committed lock matches its dependency file and installs from its hashes; freshness stays on its schedule.\r\n    - **New builds, not yet sequenced** (both reviewers): a lane-local environment built from the lock, a landing environment built from the lock, the facts script, the admission hat, the loader coupling to `decision_recording`, and the pre-review lock and audit steps. The owner-or-delegate default works today without them only because